### ⬇️ Turant Iske Niche (Part 2) Copy Karna Shuru Kar ⬇️

```markdown
---

## 🗄️ Database Schema (SQLite)

The database is structured to support workflow persistence, execution tracking, and real-time logging.

### 1. `workflows`
Stores the saved DSL definitions.
- `id` (PK, UUID)
- `name` (String)
- `description` (Text)
- `dsl_content` (Text / JSON)
- `created_at` (Timestamp)
- `updated_at` (Timestamp)

### 2. `executions`
Represents a single run of a compiled workflow.
- `id` (PK, UUID)
- `workflow_id` (FK -> workflows.id)
- `status` (Enum: pending, running, completed, failed)
- `total_tokens` (Integer)
- `total_cost` (Float)
- `started_at` (Timestamp)
- `completed_at` (Timestamp)

### 3. `execution_nodes`
Tracks the state of every node in the graph during a specific execution.
- `id` (PK, UUID)
- `execution_id` (FK -> executions.id)
- `node_id` (String - ID from the IR graph)
- `status` (Enum: pending, running, completed, failed)
- `provider_used` (String)
- `prompt_sent` (Text)
- `output_received` (Text)
- `tokens_used` (Integer)
- `latency_ms` (Integer)

### 4. `provider_configs`
Stores API keys and toggles for LLM providers.
- `id` (PK, Integer)
- `provider_name` (Enum: gemini, openai, claude)
- `api_key_encrypted` (String - stored locally)
- `is_active` (Boolean)
- `default_model` (String)

---

## 🔌 API Endpoints Reference

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|--------|----------|-------------|
| `POST` | `/api/compile` | Accepts DSL. Returns AST, IR, and Optimized Graph. |
| `POST` | `/api/execute` | Accepts an optimized graph. Starts async execution. |
| `GET` | `/api/workflows` | List all saved workflows. |
| `POST` | `/api/workflows` | Save a new workflow. |
| `GET` | `/api/executions` | List past executions. |
| `GET` | `/api/executions/{id}`| Get detailed execution metadata and node states. |
| `GET` | `/api/providers` | List configured providers. |
| `POST` | `/api/providers` | Update/Create provider configs. |
| `WS` | `/ws/execution/{id}` | WebSocket for live execution logs. |

---

## 📝 The Workflow DSL (Domain Specific Language)

The DSL is designed to be easily readable by humans and easily parsable by the compiler. It is written in JSON format.

**Example: A Chain Workflow (Write -> Summarize -> Translate)**

```json
{
  "workflow_name": "content_pipeline",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "input_1",
      "type": "input",
      "data": { "text": "The future of AI in healthcare" }
    },
    {
      "id": "writer",
      "type": "llm",
      "provider": "gemini",
      "dependencies": ["input_1"],
      "prompt": "Write a 200-word article about: {{input_1}}"
    },
    {
      "id": "summarizer",
      "type": "llm",
      "provider": "gemini",
      "dependencies": ["writer"],
      "prompt": "Summarize this into 3 bullet points: {{writer}}"
    },
    {
      "id": "translator",
      "type": "llm",
      "provider": "openai", 
      "dependencies": ["summarizer"],
      "prompt": "Translate to Hindi: {{summarizer}}"
    }
  ]
}
Note how the provider switches from Gemini to OpenAI (Mock) for the translation step, demonstrating multi-provider flexibility.

🚀 Getting Started (Local Setup)
Prerequisites
Python 3.11+

Node.js 18+

A Google Gemini API Key (Free tier is perfectly fine).

1. Clone the Repository
bash
git clone https://github.com/vishakha2121/neural-compiler.git
cd neural-compiler
2. Backend Setup (FastAPI)
bash
cd backend
python -m venv .venv
# Windows: 
.venv\Scripts\activate
# Mac/Linux:
source .venv/bin/activate

pip install -r requirements.txt

# Setup Environment variables
copy .env.example .env
# Edit .env and add your GEMINI_API_KEY
Initialize the Database:

bash
python db/init_db.py
Start the server:

bash
uvicorn main:app --reload --port 8000
API runs at http://localhost:8000. Swagger docs at http://localhost:8000/docs.

3. Frontend Setup (React + Vite)
Open a new terminal window:

bash
cd frontend
npm install

# Setup Environment variables
copy .env.example .env
# Ensure VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

npm run dev
UI runs at http://localhost:5173.

🧪 Testing
Backend Tests (Pytest)
Ensure you are in the backend directory and your virtual environment is active.

bash
pytest tests/ -v
Frontend Tests (Vitest - optional)
bash
cd frontend
npm run test
📸 Screenshots & UI Showcase
(Note: Replace these placeholders with actual screenshots of your app once completed)

Dashboard Overview	Compiler Playground
https://via.placeholder.com/600x350/1e293b/ffffff?text=Dashboard+UI	https://via.placeholder.com/600x350/1e293b/ffffff?text=Compiler+Playground
Workflow Builder (React Flow)	Live Execution Monitor
https://via.placeholder.com/600x350/1e293b/ffffff?text=Workflow+Builder	https://via.placeholder.com/600x350/1e293b/ffffff?text=Live+Logs
🎓 Learning Outcomes & Interview Talking Points
If you are reviewing this project (or if I am interviewing with you), here are the key engineering concepts demonstrated:

Compiler Construction: Implementing Lexers, Parsers, ASTs, and Semantic Analysis from scratch without relying on heavy external libraries.

Graph Optimization: Understanding Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs), topological sorting, dead code elimination, and common subexpression elimination (CSE).

Multi-Provider Abstraction: Designing clean interfaces (Adapter Pattern) to switch between different AI APIs seamlessly.

Real-time Distributed Systems: Using WebSockets to stream execution events and handle asynchronous state updates in React.

Full-Stack Architecture: Integrating a Python backend, a React frontend, and an SQLite database into a cohesive, responsive application.

UI/UX Design: Building complex interactive interfaces like drag-and-drop graphs (React Flow) and code editors (Monaco) with a premium aesthetic.

🗺️ Future Roadmap
While this is a practice project, future iterations could include:

□ Local LLM Support: Integrate Ollama to run models like Llama 3 locally (requires GPU).
□ Advanced Optimizer Passes: Add loop unrolling and branch prediction for conditionals.
□ Workflow Versioning: Track changes to workflows over time and allow rollbacks.
□ Cost Control: Set hard limits on token usage per execution to prevent runaway costs.
□ Dockerization: Provide a docker-compose.yml for one-click deployment.
⚠️ Disclaimer
This project is built purely for educational and portfolio purposes. It is explicitly not production-ready. It is designed to run on a CPU-only laptop and relies on cloud APIs for LLM inference. Do not use this for enterprise-level workloads. Keep your .env files secret and never commit API keys.

📄 License
This project is licensed under the MIT License. See the LICENSE file for details.

🙏 Acknowledgements
The Dragon Book (Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools) for the foundational concepts.

FastAPI and React teams for incredible developer experiences.

React Flow for making graph UI development so easy.

Google Gemini for providing free API access for learners.

Built with ☕, 🧠, and a deep love for compilers and AI.

