⬇️ Yahan Se Copy Karna Shuru Kar (Part 1) ⬇️
markdown
# 🧠 Neural Compiler — AI Compiler for Large Language Models

> **Transform prompts and multi-step workflows into optimized execution graphs — powered by a real compiler pipeline, multi-provider LLM adapters, and a beautiful React dashboard.**

[![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.11+-3776AB?logo=python&logoColor=white)](https://www.python.org/)
[![FastAPI](https://img.shields.io/badge/FastAPI-0.100+-009688?logo=fastapi&logoColor=white)](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/)
[![React](https://img.shields.io/badge/React-18+-61DAFB?logo=react&logoColor=black)](https://react.dev/)
[![Vite](https://img.shields.io/badge/Vite-5+-646CFF?logo=vite&logoColor=white)](https://vitejs.dev/)
[![TailwindCSS](https://img.shields.io/badge/TailwindCSS-3+-38BDF8?logo=tailwindcss&logoColor=white)](https://tailwindcss.com/)
[![SQLite](https://img.shields.io/badge/SQLite-3+-003B57?logo=sqlite&logoColor=white)](https://sqlite.org/)
[![Gemini](https://img.shields.io/badge/Gemini-API-4285F4?logo=google&logoColor=white)](https://ai.google.dev/)
[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-green.svg)](./LICENSE)

---

## 📌 Executive Summary

**Neural Compiler** is a full-stack, AI-native compiler designed to take high-level prompts and multi-step workflows and convert them into **optimized, provider-agnostic execution graphs**. 

Just like a traditional compiler (like GCC or LLVM) transforms human-readable source code into efficient machine code, this project transforms human-readable prompt workflows (DSL) into a structured **Intermediate Representation (IR)** — a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) — which is then optimized, parallelized, and executed across multiple Large Language Model (LLM) providers.

**Why does this project exist?**
Modern AI applications are rarely a single prompt. They involve multi-step reasoning chains, conditional branching, parallel calls, and cost/latency tradeoffs. Developers hand-code these workflows with fragile chains of `await` calls. There is no compiler, no optimizer, and no observability.

Neural Compiler solves this by bringing **classic compiler theory** to **modern AI workloads**. It demonstrates:
- Compiler construction (Lexer, Parser, Semantic Analyzer, IR, Optimizer)
- Graph theory (DAG optimization, topological sort, parallelization detection)
- Multi-provider abstraction (Adapter pattern for Gemini, OpenAI, Claude)
- Real-time systems (WebSockets, streaming execution logs)
- Full-stack engineering (FastAPI backend, React frontend, SQLite DB)
- Beautiful modern UI/UX (React Flow, TailwindCSS, Framer Motion)

> **Note:** This is a **practice/learning project**, explicitly built for portfolio and educational purposes. It is **not intended for enterprise or production deployment** and runs entirely on CPU (cloud API dependent).

---

## 🏗️ System Architecture & Compiler Pipeline

The project follows the classic compiler design architecture, elegantly adapted for LLMs.

```text
[ USER WORKFLOW DSL (JSON/YAML) ]
               │
               ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 1. LEXER                              │ -> Converts raw text/JSON into structured tokens.
└──────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                   ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 2. PARSER                             │ -> Builds an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) from tokens.
└──────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                   ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 3. SEMANTIC ANALYZER                  │ -> Validates dependencies, detects cycles, checks types.
└──────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                   ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 4. IR BUILDER (Intermediate Rep.)     │ -> Converts AST into a Graph-based DAG representation.
└──────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                   ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 5. OPTIMIZER ENGINE                   │ -> Performs graph-level optimizations:
│    • Dead Code Elimination            │    (Removes unused nodes)
│    • Parallelizer                     │    (Groups independent nodes)
│    • Common Subexpression (CSE)       │    (Caches identical prompts)
│    • Provider Selector                │    (Assigns best provider)
└──────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                   ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 6. EXECUTOR (DAG Traversal)           │ -> Runs the graph using topological sort & async tasks.
└──────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                   ▼
       ┌───────────┼───────────┐
       ▼           ▼           ▼
 [ Gemini ]   [ OpenAI ]  [ Claude ]
 (API Call)   (Mock API)  (Mock API)
       │           │           │
       └───────────┼───────────┘
                   ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 7. WEBSOCKET STREAMER                 │ -> Pushes live execution logs to the React Frontend.
└───────────────────────────────────────┘
✨ Detailed Features Breakdown
1. The Compiler Core (Backend)
Lexer: Tokenizes the input DSL. Handles strings, numbers, identifiers, and punctuation.

Parser: Builds an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). Includes robust error handling with line/column tracking.

Semantic Analyzer: Walks the AST to ensure all node references exist, variables are defined before use, and there are no circular dependencies (DAG validation).

IR Builder: Transforms the AST into a formal graph (Nodes & Edges).

Optimizer Passes:

Dead Code Elimination: If a node's output isn't used, it's removed.

Parallelizer: Detects sibling nodes with no dependencies and schedules them concurrently.

Common Subexpression Elimination (CSE): If two nodes use the exact same prompt and provider, it executes once and caches the result.

Provider Selection: Inspects node constraints and routes to the cheapest/fastest provider.

Executor: A custom asynchronous DAG executor. It uses topological sorting to execute nodes in the correct order, using asyncio for parallel branches.

2. Multi-Provider Adapters (Backend)
Uses the Adapter Pattern.

BaseProvider interface defines methods like generate(prompt) and estimate_tokens(prompt).

Gemini Adapter (Live): Fully implemented using Google's google-generativeai SDK.

OpenAI & Claude Adapters (Mock): Simulates responses and token counts to demonstrate multi-provider capability without needing paid API keys.

Registry: A factory pattern that returns the correct provider instance based on the optimized graph configuration.

3. API Layer & Real-time Streaming (FastAPI)
Fully documented REST API using FastAPI and Pydantic v2.

WebSockets: A dedicated WebSocket endpoint (/ws/execution/{id}) that streams execution events (Node Started, Node Completed, Error, Finished) to the frontend in real-time.

Database Integration: SQLite via SQLAlchemy ORM.

4. The React UI (Frontend)
Modern Design: Dark mode default, glassmorphism UI, Framer Motion animations, TailwindCSS styling.

Dashboard: Overview of total executions, token usage, cost estimation, and recent workflows.

Workflow Builder: A visual drag-and-drop canvas built with React Flow. Users can drag LLM nodes, connect them with edges, and configure prompts.

Compiler Playground: A split-screen view. On the left is a Monaco Editor (like VS Code) for writing the DSL. On the right, users can visually inspect the generated AST, IR, and the Optimized Graph step-by-step.

Execution Monitor: A terminal-style streaming log view. Shows which nodes are running, pending, completed, or failed in real-time, along with token counts and latency.

Provider Settings: UI to manage API keys and toggle active providers.

🛠️ Comprehensive Tech Stack
Layer	Technology	Purpose
Frontend	React 18	UI Library
Vite	Build tool & Dev server
TailwindCSS	Utility-first styling
React Flow	Interactive node-based graph editor
Zustand	Lightweight state management
Monaco Editor	Code editor for DSL
Framer Motion	UI Animations
Lucide React	Icon library
Axios	HTTP Client
Backend	Python 3.11	Core language
FastAPI	Web framework (REST + WebSockets)
Uvicorn	ASGI Server
Pydantic v2	Data validation & serialization
SQLAlchemy 2.0	ORM for Database
Google GenAI SDK	Live Gemini API integration
WebSockets	Real-time log streaming
Database	SQLite	Lightweight local storage
DevOps/Tools	Git & GitHub	Version control
Ruff	Python Linter
ESLint/Prettier	Frontend Linting
